The README quickstart walks the default case in five commands. This page walks the same
five steps slowly: what each command touches, which environment variable backs it, what
the default resolves to, and where the invocation can diverge from the default. Each step
follows one pattern:

- **control** — the abstract signature: what the verb accepts.
- **environment** — the variable behind it, and who writes it where.
- **default** — what happens when you say nothing.
- **walkthrough** — the specific invocation, with the files and logs it produces.

## 0 — install

**control**

```sh
curl -fsSL https://deno.land/install.sh | sh
git clone https://github.com/vivalence/vivalence.git
cd vivalence
deno task install
```

Deno 2.7+ is the only prerequisite; the repo carries everything else. `deno task install`
is two moves: `deno task dependencies` fetches the workspace, then `systems/ghost/install.sh`
links the CLI — `~/.deno/bin/viva` becomes a symlink to the repo's `ghost.sh`. The deno
installer already put `~/.deno/bin` on your PATH; if deno is brand new, open a fresh shell
once and both `deno` and `viva` resolve.

**environment** — `VIVA_REPOSITORY_MOUNT`, written by the installer into
`~/.config/viva/env` (XDG config, not your shell rc). Every `viva` invocation sources that
file before running, so the CLI knows its repo from anywhere and your shell configuration
stays untouched.

**walkthrough**

```sh
$ deno task install
Task dependencies deno install --allow-scripts=npm:sqlite3,npm:svelte-preprocess
Download https://registry.npmjs.org/…
Task ghost/install sh systems/ghost/install.sh "$(pwd)"
viva → /home/you/vivalence  ·  config: /home/you/.config/viva/env

$ cat ~/.config/viva/env
export VIVA_REPOSITORY_MOUNT="/home/you/vivalence"
```

One more thing rides along invisibly: `ghost.sh` exports `VIVA_PROCESS_ID` — the pid of
the shell that invoked it. That id is what later lets each terminal hold its own instance
selection ([step 4](#4--instanceuse--init--auth)).

## 1 — `viva ledger/init`

**control**

```sh
viva ledger/init [path]
```

Creates the **ledger** — the one directory Vivalence owns on your machine. Everything the
system remembers between runs lives under it: which packages are tapped, which instances
exist, what is currently running.

**environment** — `VIVA_LEDGER_MOUNT`. The wizard persists your choice into the same
`~/.config/viva/env`. Passing an explicit `[path]` skips the wizard — and skips
persisting, so an explicit path is a one-off, not a decision.

**default** — `~/.viva`.

**walkthrough**

```sh
$ viva ledger/init
# wizard: pick the home, persist VIVA_LEDGER_MOUNT

$ tree ~/.viva
~/.viva/
  .env                                  the machine's own environment file, scaffolded from its schema
  instances.json     {}                 known instances on this machine
  instances/                            the shelf — created instances land here
  registry/                             the store — remote packages clone here
  locks/                                what is running
  logs/                                 per-instance streams
  sessions/                             per-shell selections
```

It is machine state, never git-managed at the top level — its *children* are the git
units. `viva ledger/doctor` prints the full report card at any time: homes, record, store
census, locks, sessions, and every environment variable with the voice that won it.
[[33.01_anatomy-of-the-ledger]] is the deep anatomy.

## 2 — `viva registry/tap`

**control**

```sh
viva registry/tap <source: path | git url> [target: path]
viva registry/untap <reference>
```

Adds a **package** to the registry. Packages are modes whose job is to carry other modes:
datasets, domains, services, instance recipes. A local path is recorded in place; a git
URL is cloned into the ledger's store — or into `[target]` if you'd rather keep it
somewhere of your own. Either way the package lands in the record and its modes become
referenceable.

**environment** — `VIVA_REGISTRY_MOUNT` moves the store.

**default** — the store is `<ledger>/registry/`; a remote tap without `[target]` clones
into it.

**walkthrough**

```sh
$ viva registry/tap https://github.com/vivalence/registry-education

$ cat ~/.viva/registry.json
[
  "/home/you/vivalence/registry/viva",
  "/home/you/vivalence/registry/fixtures",
  "/home/you/vivalence/registry/playground",
  <!-- "registry-education" -->
]

$ ls ~/.viva/registry/registry-education
education.viva.js  domain/  modes/  topologies/  topographies/
```

What a package looks like — `@viva/viva`, the standard library:

```
registry/viva/
  package.viva.js            manifest: { owner: "@viva", type: "package", slug: "viva" }
  datamaps/libsql/           database provider
  lighthouses/multiplayer/   identity service
  hallucinators/anthropic/   AI providers
  hallucinators/elevenlabs/
  services/nlp/
  instances/hello-world/     runnable instance recipes
  instances/starter/
```

Every leaf is a mode with its own `*.viva.js`; the package is the one module at the root
that claims the tree. A mode is referenced by owner/type/slug — `@viva/instance/hello-world`
— where the type comes from the manifest, never the directory name.

To start a package rather than adopt one, bootstrap it. With no source you get a bare
manifest; with one, that package's tree is cloned and its declaration rewritten to the
destination — the directory name becomes the slug, and the slug at-signed becomes the
owner. Either way the result is tapped. An ambiguous source opens the picker.

```sh
viva registry/bootstrap ~/code/mine
viva registry/bootstrap ~/code/mine viva
```

## 3 — `viva instance/create`

**control**

```sh
viva instance/create <source: @owner/instance/slug | path> [target: path] [--slug=<name>] [--use]
```

An **instance** is one runnable system: a runtime, its daemons, its clients — declared in
one directory. `create` copies the recipe out of the registry. It is a full copy, yours to
edit and version: the declaration — which carries its own `environment` schema — and, once
running, its `mountpoint/` with databases and bundles. Two runs of the same recipe are two
shelved copies, not two moods of one.

**environment** — `VIVA_INSTANCE_MOUNT` names the one instance a runtime renders — and a
`*_MOUNT` is always a **path, never a slug**. Paladin throws on a slug rather than
resolving one; the slug→path lookup belongs to the operator verbs.

**default** — no target shelves the copy under `<ledger>/instances/<slug>` and records it
in `instances.json`. The slug defaults to the recipe's; `--slug=<name>` names the copy
yourself, and an already-held slug is a hard error — no suffixing, pick another.

**bind** — `--use` selects the new copy for this shell the moment it lands, and records it
on the shelf: `create --use` is `create` followed by the `instances/use` of section 4.

**walkthrough**

```sh
$ viva instance/create @viva/instance/hello-world

$ ls ~/.viva/instances/hello-world
hello-world.viva.js  education.js  playground.js

$ cat ~/.viva/instances.json
{
  "hello-world": {
    "mount": "/home/you/.viva/instances/hello-world",
    "createdAt": "…"
  }
}
```

Note what the instance directory does *not* contain: its own location. An instance cannot
declare where it lives — you needed its location to read its files, so storing it inside
would be circular. Location is the ledger's knowledge.

## 4 — `viva instances/use` · `init` · `auth`

**control**

```sh
viva instances/use <slug> [--ledger]
viva instance/init
viva instance/auth signup <username> <password>
```

**environment** — the selection `use` writes ends up as `VIVA_INSTANCE_MOUNT`, resolved
to a path. Per shell it lives in `<ledger>/sessions/<shell-pid>.json` (the
`VIVA_PROCESS_ID` from step 0); with `--ledger` it is upserted as one line into
`<ledger>/.env`, the machine's own environment file.

**default** — nothing is selected. Every instance verb resolves its target the same way,
and the voices have a fixed precedence — strongest first:

```
flag      --instance=<slug|path>              this invocation only
cwd       standing inside an instance directory
os        an exported VIVA_INSTANCE_MOUNT
session   viva instances/use <slug>            this shell
ledger    viva instances/use <slug> --ledger   this machine
```

This is why the controls diverge instead of duplicating: `--instance` targets one command,
the directory you stand in targets implicitly, `use` makes a shell decision, `--ledger`
makes a machine decision. Two terminals can `use` two instances and work them in parallel.
Bare `viva instances/use` prints the current selection and which voice won it;
`viva ledger/doctor` prints the same for every variable, shadowed voices dimmed.
The full seven-strata ladder — these plus the instance's own files — is
[[33.01_anatomy-of-the-ledger]].

**walkthrough**

```sh
$ viva instances/use hello-world
# trailing segments chain under /instance: viva instances/use hello-world init

$ cat ~/.viva/sessions/48213.json
{ "VIVA_INSTANCE_MOUNT": "/home/you/.viva/instances/hello-world" }

$ viva instance/init
# reads the declaration's `environment` schema and prompts for what is still owed

$ viva instance/auth signup you changeme
```

`init` runs first-boot. There is one environment file — `.env`, never committed — and one
schema, the declaration's own `environment` export, which describes every key it needs.
`init` reconciles the two, prompts for what is missing, and writes `.env`. Defaults may
reference each other with `${VAR}`, expanded on read, so nine addresses derive from one
origin and moving to a real host is a single edit. Secrecy is decided by the **key**: a
`SECRET_*` goes to the secret store wherever it is written. Then `auth signup`
boots the instance headless, registers your account against its lighthouse — the identity
service; there is no signup UI — and shuts down again. Repeat it any time for more users.

## 5 — `viva instance/run`

**control**

```sh
viva instance/run
viva instance/stop
```

Boots the instance's children as supervised processes and registers each in the ledger's
locks — `<instance>_<process>.lock`, holding the pid.

**walkthrough**

```sh
$ viva instance/run

$ ls ~/.viva/locks
hello-world_runtime.lock  hello-world_kajuit.lock
```

For `hello-world` that is two processes:

| process   | what                    | where                   |
| --------- | ----------------------- | ----------------------- |
| `runtime` | daemons, entities, HTTP | `http://localhost:2501` |
| `kajuit`  | the browser client      | `http://localhost:1794` |

The runtime hosts everything as one path tree under one origin: daemons under
`/daemon/<slug>`, attached services like the lighthouse under `/attached/process/…`, mode
bundles under `/attached/bundle/…`.

Open **http://localhost:1794**, log in with the account from step 4, and you are inside:
the kajuit client attaches to the daemons and renders their modes as buffers.

